---
import { contacts } from '../data/projects';

/* The site's one footer, as a component (2026-10-12, Hiro). Until now every
   route ended on the same copyright-only line, written out four times (home,
   about, project pages, 404). On a phone that made About and the project
   pages dead ends: the nav's page and contact links live behind the ≡ below
   640px, so a visitor who had just finished reading — the likeliest moment to
   reach out — had no way onward without scrolling back to the top. This puts
   the same destinations at the bottom of every page, at every width.

   Same box as the four it replaces — max-w-[960px]/px-5 so it sits on the
   content column's left edge, py-10, text-xs on --ink-soft — so the copyright
   reads exactly as before. The links are .btn-quiet, the nav's own text-link
   family (same ink, hover and underline), at the footer's size rather than the
   bar's.

   Pages first, then contacts, with a wider gap between the two groups rather
   than a drawn divider. The contacts come from `contacts`, the list the nav
   reads, so an address changes in one place. Email is a plain mailto: here at
   every width; the nav's copy-on-desktop gesture belongs to its hover pill,
   which the footer doesn't have. External links open a tab, as in the nav.

   THE ↗ ON THE CONTACTS ONLY (same day, Hiro). It is the site's mark for
   "this takes you somewhere else" — every row of the phone menu wears one,
   and so does a project page's link out — and the footer's contacts leave
   for a tab or the mail app without it, so they were the one place breaking
   that rule. Work and About stay on the site and go unmarked, which also
   tells the two groups apart without a divider. The glyph is LinkOut's
   plain ↗, not the menu's boxed one: these are .btn-quiet links, and that is
   the family's arrow. Words, not icons, before it — icon-only contacts were
   turned down on 08-15 for making a visitor recognise a bird to find
   Twitter, and icon + word + ↗ four times was too busy for the footer.

   aria-current mirrors the nav's: "page" on About when you are on it, "true"
   on Work from a project page. */
const path = Astro.url.pathname;
const pages = [
	{ href: '/#projects', label: 'Work', current: path.startsWith('/projects/') ? 'true' : undefined },
	{ href: '/about', label: 'About', current: path === '/about' || path === '/about/' ? 'page' : undefined },
];
---

<footer class="site-footer mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-10 text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
	<nav aria-label="Footer" class="site-footer__nav">
		<ul class="site-footer__group" role="list">
			{pages.map((p) => (
				<li>
					<a href={p.href} class="btn-quiet site-footer__link" aria-current={p.current}>
						<span class="btn-quiet__label">{p.label}</span>
					</a>
				</li>
			))}
		</ul>
		<ul class="site-footer__group" role="list">
			{contacts.map((c) => {
				const isEmail = c.href.startsWith('mailto:');
				return (
					<li>
						<a
							href={c.href}
							class="btn-quiet site-footer__link"
							target={isEmail ? undefined : '_blank'}
							rel="noopener"
						>
							<span class="btn-quiet__label">{c.label}</span>
							<svg
								class="site-footer__ext"
								viewBox="0 0 24 24"
								fill="none"
								stroke="currentColor"
								stroke-width="2"
								stroke-linecap="round"
								stroke-linejoin="round"
								aria-hidden="true"
							>
								<line x1="5" y1="19" x2="19" y2="5"></line>
								<polyline points="9 5 19 5 19 15"></polyline>
							</svg>
						</a>
					</li>
				);
			})}
		</ul>
	</nav>
	<span>© 2026 Hiro Eernisse</span>
</footer>
